So the `gateway` tool has `ownerOnly: true`. Let me look at how `ownerOnly` tools are handled. Looking at `applyOwnerOnlyToolPolicy` in `tool-policy.ts`:```typescript
export function applyOwnerOnlyToolPolicy(tools: AnyAgentTool[], senderIsOwner: boolean) {
  const withGuard = tools.map((tool) => {
    if (!isOwnerOnlyTool(tool)) {
      return tool;
    }
    return wrapOwnerOnlyToolExecution(tool, senderIsOwner);
  });
  if (senderIsOwner) {
    return withGuard;
  }
  return withGuard.filter((tool) => !isOwnerOnlyTool(tool));  // This filters OUT owner-only tools when sender is NOT owner
}
```

So when `senderIsOwner` is false, owner-only tools are completely filtered out! This means:
- In webchat sessions, the sender is likely the owner (senderIsOwner = true), so gateway tool is available
- In Feishu sessions, the sender is NOT the owner (senderIsOwner = false), so gateway tool is filtered out

BUT - if `tools.alsoAllow: ["gateway"]` is set, the gateway tool should be added to the allowed list. 

The issue is that `alsoAllow` gets applied during the profile resolution stage to prevent tools from being filtered out early, but `applyOwnerOnlyToolPolicy` runs afterward and can still remove them. I need to trace where `applyOwnerOnlyToolPolicy` is actually called to understand the full flow.

